index 0000000..a7e0f91
--- /dev/null
+++ b/pyto/experiments/grouped-ablation/candidates/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,89 @@
+# Retained-program schema candidates: three shapes, three lenses
+
+Generated by `candidates/refute.py`; `python3 candidates/refute.py --check` (run by
+test_retain.py) fails if this file and a fresh lens run disagree. Every verdict and
+number below was produced by that run.
+
+## Fixtures
+
+- `day1` -- the Day 1 grouped ablation: fn: refs everywhere, every invocation publishes
+- `fanout_px` -- a px-only fan-out (second PCR over the same PxC), every invocation publishes
+- `no_into` -- one invocation with into=None: a result kept as fn: and never published
+
+The `day1` program holds 15 invocations carrying 24 `fn:` direct-result bindings; `fanout_px` holds 2 px-only invocations; `no_into` holds 1 invocation with `into=None`.
+
+## Verdicts
+
+| candidate | roundtrip | expressiveness | leakage |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| A. testimony-shaped | accept | accept | accept |
+| B. graph.Pcr JSON-shaped | reject | accept | accept |
+| C. browser readPql-shaped | reject | reject | accept |
+
+## Why each verdict
+
+### A. testimony-shaped
+
+Shape: `{name, ticks:[{name, calculations:[{id, calculation, inputs, args, into}]}]}`
+
+- **roundtrip: accept** -- day1: testimony bytes equal; entries identical to testimony; fanout_px: testimony bytes equal; entries identical to testimony
+- **expressiveness: accept** -- 24 fn: ref(s) held; 1 invocation(s) with into=None held
+- **leakage: accept** -- day1: json-serialisable, no lambda/<function; fanout_px: json-serialisable, no lambda/<function
+
+### B. graph.Pcr JSON-shaped
+
+Shape: `{PrincipleComponentRender, Ticks:[{name, Calculations:[{id, call, with:{k:{kind,ref}}, args, into?}]}]}`
+
+- **roundtrip: reject** -- day1: restore refused (PcrJsonNotExecutable); fanout_px: restore refused (PcrJsonNotExecutable)
+- **expressiveness: accept** -- 24 fn: ref(s) held; 1 invocation(s) with into=None held
+- **leakage: accept** -- day1: json-serialisable, no lambda/<function; fanout_px: json-serialisable, no lambda/<function
+
+### C. browser readPql-shaped
+
+Shape: `{PrincipleComponentRender, Ticks:[{name, Calculations:[{call, with:{k:address}, args, into}]}]}`
+
+- **roundtrip: reject** -- day1: export refused (PqlDocumentError); fanout_px: testimony bytes DIFFER; entries need translation
+- **expressiveness: reject** -- 24 fn: ref(s): refused (PqlDocumentError); 1 invocation(s) with into=None: refused (PqlDocumentError)
+- **leakage: accept** -- day1: not exportable (PqlDocumentError); fanout_px: json-serialisable, no lambda/<function
+
+## Survivor
+
+Accepted by all three lenses: A. testimony-shaped.
+
+The expected survivor was the testimony shape and it survived, but not for the reason
+the plan assumed. The decisive lens is `roundtrip`, not `leakage`: all three shapes hold
+data only, so the leakage lens does not discriminate and is weak evidence on its own
+(it is kept because it is the lens that would catch a regression, not because it chose).
+
+`graph.Pcr` exposes an executing or importing method: False. Checked
+at run time with `hasattr`, so candidate B's refusal rests on a fact and not only on a
+quotation of docs/PYTHON-LAB-STEWARDSHIP.md:19.
+
+The leakage lens accepted all three because the check that matters happens before any of
+them: `retain.to_program` refuses a callable in `args` at export, naming the invocation --
+`RetainError: retain: invocation 'leak' arg 'post' is a function, which JSON cannot describe; retained data holds no executable code and no repr placeholders (docs/PYTHON-LAB-STEWARDSHIP.md:39)`
+
+Candidate C is not merely lossy: the readPql grammar has no field for an invocation id
+(src/core/exec.js:46 builds `{call, with, args, into}`), and `fn:` refs are addressed by
+id, so the shape cannot express them even in principle. Restoring one therefore has to
+invent ids, and the replayed testimony differs from the original on the `id` field alone.
+
+## The same documents through the real browser reader
+
+`candidates/readpql_check.mjs` imports `readPql`/`invokePql` from `src/core/exec.js`
+(relative path, inside this repository) with `JSON.parse` as the parser.
+
+| case | kind | pass | what node reported |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| px_only_fanout_accepted | accept | True | read 1 tick(s), 2 calculation(s) |
+| missing_into_refused | reject | True | PQL T.Calculations[0].into: expected a nonempty string. |
+| shadowed_arg_refused | reject | True | PQL T.Calculations[0]: 'v' appears in both with and args. |
+| fn_ref_written_as_address_is_read_then_fails_at_invoke | invoke_fails | True | PQL Fit: fn.ablation.fit -> scratch.ablation.model.all failed. \| exec board: slot 'fn:split' not produced yet. |
+
+All node cases passed: True.
+
+The last row is the finding that put the `fn:` refusal in Python rather than in the
+grammar: node's reader accepts `"split": "fn:split"` as an ordinary Part address, so a
+silently-degraded document would be read successfully and die only at invocation. That
+is why `pql_document.to_pql_document` raises `PqlDocumentError` naming the first
+offending invocation instead of writing the literal address.
